WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.671 --> 00:00:07.290
Oh, my God.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.190
Marvel finally gave us a new trailer

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:11.290
for "Spider-Man: Far From Home," and

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:13.390
compared to the initial tease, it really

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:16.100
sets up a post-"Endgame" MCU.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.080
Nick Fury: Beck is from
Earth, just not ours.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.980
The snap tore a hole in our dimension.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.110
Spider-Man: You're saying
there's a multiverse?

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:25.520
Matt: Yes, that's exactly
what the trailer is saying.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:28.380
In the Marvel comics, there
exists the multiverse.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.410
It's a way to tell alternate stories

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:32.380
about characters you're
already familiar with

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.810
that diverge from the
main Marvel continuity,

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:37.373
but it doesn't affect the
main Marvel continuity.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.730
Basically, the multiverse
is a way of creating

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:46.430
and working with ongoing
alternate realities.

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.670
Think of each reality as its own universe

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.130
with its own timeline.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.210
Put them together, and
they're the multiverse.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:54.820
If you saw "Spider-Man:
Into the Spider-Verse,"

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.950
you're already familiar with the concept.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:59.840
Each universe has its own
quirks with its own heroes.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.330
Sometimes there are
overwhelming similarities,

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:05.140
but usually there are huge differences.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.930
For example, the Marvel universe

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.860
in the comics is referred to as Earth 616.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.490
This is the one that has
existed for 80 years,

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:14.590
and aside from the MCU, it's the one

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:16.270
that most people are familiar with.

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:18.840
But if a creator wants to
tell a different story,

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.420
say, one where Charles Xavier is murdered

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.570
at a young age, causing a chain reaction

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:24.930
that sees the villain Apocalypse

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:27.090
conquer almost the entire world,

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:28.730
then an alternate universe is needed.

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:31.710
Give it a number, in this case Earth 295,

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:32.940
and you're good to go.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.840
Tell your story, do whatever you want

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.390
to the characters, and none of it

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.780
should affect the main timeline,
but more on that later.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.880
Now, time travel can and does

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.540
play a part in these realities.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.930
The X-Men are constantly
traveling through time

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.930
to avert possible disaster,
whether it's from Sentinels

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.290
in the legendary "Days
of Future Past" story

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:53.340
or in the more recent "Old Man Logan."

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.970
Both storylines took
place in dystopian futures

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:59.110
with X-Men from present-day Earth 616,

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.620
but the future events
of "Days of Future Past"

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.760
took place on Earth 811,
while the future events

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:09.760
of "Old Man Logan" took
place on Earth 807,128.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.540
It's a way of working with characters you

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:13.380
already know and love while adding

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.390
all sorts of twists,
turns, and alternate takes.

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:18.550
Hey, I know they later
changed "Old Man Logan"

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:20.740
to not be the X-Man
we're all familiar with,

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.790
but we'll save retroactive
continuity for another video.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.210
Now, remember that part
about characters from

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:29.240
other timelines not
affecting the main timeline?

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.790
Well, that actually gets
a little complicated.

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:33.370
If a character or something else

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:35.500
from an alternate reality is a hit,

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.940
say, the extremely evil version of Beast

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:39.870
from the "Age of Apocalypse," then they'll

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:42.380
usually somehow cross
through time and space

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:46.190
to wind up in the main
Marvel reality, Earth 616.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:48.440
Characters from one reality can and do

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:51.280
cross over to other
realities all the time.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:53.900
One comic, "The Exiles," sees characters

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.690
from various realities come together

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:58.630
as a team to hop from
one reality to another,

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:01.040
averting disaster and
correcting huge mistakes

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:02.110
in the timeline.

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:03.500
Every single one of their missions

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.340
could be considered a time heist.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.340
Were you asking yourself
how Captain America

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.800
didn't screw up the timeline
at the end of "Endgame"?

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.250
He probably did.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.470
There's a good chance him staying behind

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:16.740
in the past created a whole
new dimension of reality.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.360
The main Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.330
is referred to as Earth 199,999.

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:23.840
He could've gone back and stayed behind

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.750
in what would become, say, Earth 302,481.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:30.420
He would've had to cross
over to the main MCU reality

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.970
to bring Sam the shield,
but he just kind of

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:35.290
appears on that bench
at the end of "Endgame."

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:36.800
So, it is possible.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:38.870
The Russo brothers have
been pretty adamant

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.510
this Cap came from another dimension,

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:43.700
and since there exists a team of heroes

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:46.740
whose job it is to fix
mistakes in the timeline,

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:49.770
it may be possible we'll see
the reality-hopping Exiles

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.060
on the big screen.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:52.960
Marvel could be setting up the multiverse

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:55.370
as a way to bring the Avengers, the X-Men,

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:58.610
and the Fantastic Four all
together on the big screen.

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:00.770
Having each team inhabit its own reality

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:02.340
would explain why they've never run

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.320
into each other before, and it would also

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:07.880
explain why, say, the X-Men
didn't help defeat Thanos.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:09.700
Now, there's a lot of other stuff

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:11.490
that goes along with the multiverse.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.520
It's too complicated to get in here,

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:15.030
but you should know that Merlin,

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:17.680
yes, that Merlin, or his daughter Roma

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.720
generally serves as the
protector of the multiverse.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.640
The X-Men offshoot team Excalibur

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:24.760
is very linked to the multiverse,

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:27.110
and Reed Richards of the Fantastic Four

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.080
is constantly building transporters

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:31.430
to jump through time and hop dimensions.

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:33.170
So the potential is there

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:35.060
to bring the teams together this way.

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:36.830
I'm not saying that's how they'll do it,

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:38.500
but after introducing the concept

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.950
of time travel in "Endgame" and bringing

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.740
in the multiverse in "Far From Home,"

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:45.010
it's clear Marvel has decided one universe

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:46.240
isn't enough to play with.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:47.850
They're going for infinity.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:51.084
The Ancient One: You think
you know how the world works?

00:04:51.084 --> 00:04:52.475
Stephen Strange: This
doesn't make any sense.

00:04:52.475 --> 00:04:55.478
The Ancient One: Not everything
does. Not everything has to.

